WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.815
Load balancing multiple local LLM servers can improve total request throughput,

00:00:04.815 --> 00:00:06.740
availability, and workflow flexibility.

00:00:06.740 --> 00:00:11.300
It gives tools one stable endpoint while backend servers can be added, removed, repaired,

00:00:11.300 --> 00:00:12.000
or resized.

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:13.700
The durable expectation is simple:

00:00:13.700 --> 00:00:17.570
ordinary load balancing routes requests across independently running endpoints;

00:00:17.570 --> 00:00:21.740
it does not automatically combine machines into one faster single inference session.

00:00:21.740 --> 00:00:24.505
A reliable setup depends on understanding each backend.

00:00:24.505 --> 00:00:29.210
Hardware resources, model files, runtime settings, context length, API compatibility,

00:00:29.210 --> 00:00:32.100
streaming behavior, and failure modes all shape capacity.

00:00:32.100 --> 00:00:33.850
Routing should reflect that reality.

00:00:33.850 --> 00:00:37.345
Round-robin may be enough for similar servers, while weighted, least-connections,

00:00:37.345 --> 00:00:39.220
health-check, model-aware, queue-based,

00:00:39.220 --> 00:00:42.360
and failover strategies become important as workloads diverge.

00:00:42.360 --> 00:00:44.580
Workload awareness keeps the service usable.

00:00:44.580 --> 00:00:49.020
Interactive chat, coding agents, batch summarization, document analysis, embeddings,

00:00:49.020 --> 00:00:53.240
long-context requests, and tool-using workflows have different latency, state, retry,

00:00:53.240 --> 00:00:54.460
and concurrency needs.

00:00:54.460 --> 00:00:57.480
Sticky sessions may matter when state or cache behavior matters.

00:00:57.480 --> 00:01:00.560
Stateless requests may route freely to any compatible backend.

00:01:00.560 --> 00:01:02.935
Operations and security complete the design.

00:01:02.935 --> 00:01:05.925
Measure real workloads, monitor the gateway and every backend,

00:01:05.925 --> 00:01:08.610
watch queue growth and failures, preserve streaming behavior,

00:01:08.610 --> 00:01:13.235
and troubleshoot mismatches in model names, runtime versions, endpoints, and timeouts.

00:01:13.235 --> 00:01:17.140
Protect local LLM endpoints because they may process sensitive prompts, documents,

00:01:17.140 --> 00:01:18.630
source code, and tool outputs.

00:01:18.630 --> 00:01:23.020
The goal is a practical local inference service that is measured, reliable,

00:01:23.020 --> 00:01:23.600
and safe to use.
